<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kamares ware</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2000–1700 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kamares ware is a fine Minoan pottery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protopalatial-period">Protopalatial period</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, named after the Kamares cave on Mount Ida where it was first found. Made on the wheel with walls as thin as eggshell, it is painted in white, red and orange on a dark ground with spirals and plant motifs. It was produced mainly at the palaces of Phaistos and Knossos and exported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamares-ware.jpg" alt="Kamares ware" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Sauber · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AMI_-_Kamaresvase_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
